```json
{
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "description": "List and read all project documents to understand the RICH detector requirements, candidate radiator specifications, and evaluation criteria. Focus on candidate_specs.md to identify the three radiator candidates and their properties."
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "description": "Read documentation about the simulation tool to understand how to evaluate pion/kaon separation performance and how radiator thickness affects the Cherenkov angle resolution and particle identification."
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "description": "For each of the three candidate radiators, check the transparency loss after 10-year-equivalent humidity ageing test against the hard requirement (must not exceed 10%). Eliminate any candidates that fail this constraint."
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "description": "For the remaining viable radiator candidates, systematically evaluate pion/kaon separation at 10 GeV/c across different thicknesses in the allowed range (1-6 cm). Record the separation values for each radiator-thickness combination."
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "description": "Identify which radiator-thickness combinations meet the minimum requirement of 3.5 sigma pion/kaon separation at 10 GeV/c. Among those that pass, determine which configuration achieves the best (highest) separation to meet the goal of optimal performance."
    }
  ]
}
```
